<BlogPost title="The Art of Shipping Solo Projects" date="2026-06-01">
  <p>
    Building alone has no standup, no code review, and no one to notice when you've spent a week polishing something nobody asked for. The only deadline is the one you invent, and the only reviewer is future you, wondering where the time went.
  </p>

  <h2>Perfectionism is the quiet killer</h2>
  <p>
    Early on I'd rewrite the database schema before writing a single feature. It felt like progress. It wasn't. The work that moved projects forward was almost always the ugly first version I shipped and then improved — not the polished thing I kept deferring.
  </p>

  <h2>Ship early, iterate often</h2>
  <p>
    These days I aim for the smallest thing that works and put it in front of myself (or someone else) as fast as possible. A rough draft you can click beats a perfect plan you're afraid to start. Solo doesn't mean slow — it means the loop is yours to tighten.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you're shipping alone: lower the bar for version one. You can always make it better once it exists.
  </p>
</BlogPost>
